WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:09.000
Your lab has the same cleaning code copied into fifteen scripts, each one slightly different.

2
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.060
When results disagree, nobody can say which copy ran.

3
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.600
An R package fixes that.

4
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.450
It gives shared code one home, one version number, documentation, and tests.

5
00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:23.230
Everyone installs the same tested functions, and every analysis can say exactly which version it used.

6
00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:27.180
A package is a folder with a fixed shape.

7
00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:33.560
The DESCRIPTION file holds the metadata: the name, the version, the authors, the license, and the other packages it depends on.

8
00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:35.440
The R/ folder holds your functions.

9
00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:41.180
Our example package is called labtools, and it starts with two functions, clean_site() and summarise_arm().

10
00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:49.880
Organize the R/ folder by topic: one file for each family of functions, and a utils.R file for small internal helpers.

11
00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:52.600
Package files should only define functions.

12
00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:59.740
Never put top-level code there, like library() calls or analysis steps, because that code runs every time the package is built.

13
00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:04.280
The NAMESPACE file decides what users can see.

14
00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:06.600
Exported functions are the public interface.

15
00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:09.460
Everything else stays internal, so you can change it freely.

16
00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:13.700
Here, clean_site() is exported, but its helper, .normalise(), is not.

17
00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:17.460
Users call clean_site(), and the package calls the helper behind the scenes.

18
00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:21.540
You never edit the NAMESPACE by hand.

19
00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:27.760
Instead, you add an @export tag to the function's documentation comment, and roxygen2 writes the file for you.

20
00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:30.820
Keeping the public interface small is a design decision.

21
00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:34.020
Every exported function is a promise you will have to keep.

22
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:39.910
Roxygen comments start with #', right above the function.

23
00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.030
The first line becomes the title.

24
00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:47.510
@param documents each argument, @return describes the output, and @examples shows working code.

25
00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:52.650
Run devtools::document(), and roxygen2 writes a help file into the man/ folder, along with the NAMESPACE.

26
00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:56.790
Documentation and code must agree.

27
00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:05.950
If summarise_arm() has an argument for dropping missing values (na_rm), but its documentation never mentions it, R CMD check reports a warning: undocumented arguments.

28
00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.150
Treat that as a bug.

29
00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:10.810
Someone reading the help page has no way to know the argument exists.

30
00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:15.740
Dependencies are declared in two places, for two reasons.

31
00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:20.180
The Imports field in DESCRIPTION makes sure dplyr is installed along with your package.

32
00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:23.640
The NAMESPACE decides which of its functions your code can call.

33
00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:29.320
Use @importFrom tags for the few functions you need, or write the package name, two colons, and the function.

34
00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.100
Forget one, and the check tells you.

35
00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:38.500
"No visible global function definition for group_by" means your code calls a function the package never imported.

36
00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:43.940
Even functions that ship with R need importing: sd() lives in stats, not base.

37
00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:50.300
Bare column names inside dplyr verbs raise a note too, and the .data pronoun from rlang fixes it.

38
00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:55.420
Tests turn your expectations into code that runs on every change.

39
00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:59.000
testthat files live in tests/testthat/, one for each source file.

40
00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:05.120
Each test states what should happen: clean_site() standardises codes, keeps missing values, and handles empty input.

41
00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:09.320
When someone edits the helper later, a failing test says exactly what broke.

42
00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:12.840
Before you share it, build and check.

43
00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:15.660
R CMD build bundles the source into a tarball.

44
00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:22.780
R CMD check then installs that tarball, runs every example and every test, and audits the documentation, the imports, and the code.

45
00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:25.410
devtools::check() runs the same steps from inside R.

46
00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:29.260
Aim for zero errors, zero warnings, and zero notes.

47
00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:32.370
One honest caveat.

48
00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:36.660
R CMD check needs a full R installation with a shell, and a browser does not have one.

49
00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:45.750
In RQuest, your package is built in the browser with roxygen2, and its NAMESPACE, help files, examples, and imports are checked with R's own tools.

50
00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:48.650
Run the real check on your own machine before every release.

51
00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.380
Finally, version every release.

52
00:03:52.550 --> 00:04:00.220
Semantic versioning tells colleagues what changed: a patch for bug fixes, a minor version for new features, and a major version when existing code may break.

53
00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:09.220
Record changes in a NEWS file, tag the release in git, and cite the exact package version in every analysis, so any result can be traced to the code that produced it.

54
00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:13.050
Your Package Factory missions follow the same path.

55
00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:14.810
First, export the right functions.

56
00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:18.390
Next, silence the check warnings with proper documentation and imports.

57
00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:28.560
Then ship labtools version 0.1.0, with two exported functions, complete documentation, runnable examples, and no undefined globals.
